## Verdict

- Germany: Conditional pass. The main routes work, but the register overstates archive reliability and has unresolved evidence gaps around ZDF and WELT.
- US: Fail pending correction. The NYT plan makes an unsupported general claim, and the live-blog fallback has not been shown to be sufficiently rich or enumerable.

## Findings

| # | Finding | Evidence, commands, and URLs | Severity | Suggested fix |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Required local curl reproduction was not reproducible in this environment. | Command used: `curl -L --compressed -A 'Mozilla/5.0 ... Chrome/131...' URL`. All external hosts failed with DNS error `Could not resolve host`. Web fetching confirmed RND and ZDF content, but WELT was blocked by robots and the archive/Yahoo URLs could not be independently replayed. | High | Record fetch date, UA, HTTP status, body-word count, event terms, and date for every test. Do not mark a test “ok” unless another operator can reproduce it. |
| 2 | RND’s route description understates the direct-access defect. | The register says direct access is 403 and puts Wayback first, while the notes say “zero blockers.” The live RND page does contain the event article and date, including “18.09.2024” and pager-explosion text: [RND article](https://www.rnd.de/politik/pager-explosionen-im-libanon-funkgeraete-wohl-mit-sprengstoff-bestueckt-7GX5G5II65JA5OFN6DWYOR24LI.html). | Medium | Change the country summary to “one material direct-access blocker” and label browser automation as a contingency, not a tested route. |
| 3 | Bild’s fallback is too optimistic. | The register explicitly reports “only 16 of 33 days” but still lists `wayback` as fallback. Its notes call the archive page “fetch-verified,” but that does not establish article-page recoverability for the missing 17 days. Current [Bild robots.txt](https://www.bild.de/robots.txt) also blocks `ia_archiver` with `Disallow: /`. | High | Make the dated archive page plus live sitemap the fallback/enumeration route. Mark Wayback as partial and quantify expected loss. |
| 4 | WELT’s test does not validate the claimed article route. | The test URL is a video page, while the note says to “pick article-type test at enumeration.” The current [WELT robots.txt](https://www.welt.de/robots.txt) puts `ia_archiver` under a `Disallow: /` group. The web fetcher was denied by robots for the test URL. | Medium | Add a separate article URL test with body text and event-date checks. Keep video and article coverage separate. |
| 5 | The ZDF host finding is not sufficiently demonstrated. | The current ZDFheute page is live and has event text dated `19.09.2024`: [ZDFheute article](https://www.zdfheute.de/politik/ausland/libanon-pager-explosionen-israel-100.html). Search also finds another event article dated `18.09.2024`: [Pager-Explosionen verletzen Tausende](https://www.zdfheute.de/politik/ausland/hisbollah-pager-explosionen-100.html). However, the register provides only one claimed `zdf.de/nachrichten` day-of capture, not the required second replay URL, and the “zero zdfheute captures before October 2025” assertion was not independently demonstrated here. | Medium | Add two exact Wayback replay URLs for separate `zdf.de/nachrichten` articles and an explicit zero-result query for `zdfheute.de` during Sep 15–Oct 17. |
| 6 | “All German outlets allow general crawling of articles” is too broad. | [RND robots.txt](https://www.rnd.de/robots.txt) contains an explicit automated-access prohibition and §44b reservation. [t-online robots.txt](https://www.t-online.de/robots.txt) does the same. [WELT robots.txt](https://www.welt.de/robots.txt) blocks `ia_archiver` site-wide. These are not ordinary “crawl allowed” conditions for the planned automated collection. | Medium | Separate “browser-readable,” “generic bot permitted,” “archive bot permitted,” and “TDM reservation” into different fields. |
| 7 | The NYT claim about standard articles is generalized from too little evidence. | The notes claim the flagship article has no captures until Dec 2025 and then generalize to standard articles. The requested availability checks for 2–3 additional Sep 17–20 standard URLs are absent. The notes also say CDX is blocked, so the plan needs explicit per-URL API evidence. | High | Test at least three named standard article URLs with `http://archive.org/wayback/available?url=...&timestamp=20240918`. Report each result separately. Phrase the conclusion as “these tested URLs,” not “standard articles” generally. |
| 8 | NYT live-blog coverage is not shown to be rich enough or broad enough. | The evidence cites one live-blog URL and one 6,018-word capture. That proves one rich capture, not “multiple live-blog URLs captured in-window.” | High | Enumerate several distinct NYT `/live/` URLs, record capture timestamps within Sep 15–Oct 17, and provide word counts plus event-content checks for each. |
| 9 | Reuters’ robots claim conflates robots policy with HTTP access. | The direct Reuters robots fetch was unavailable through the web fetcher. A current parsed copy shows AI crawler groups with `Disallow: /`, but the `User-agent: *` group has path-specific rules rather than blanket `Disallow: /`: [Reuters robots snapshot](https://well-known.dev/resources/robots_txt/sites/reuters.com). The register’s “unlisted agents blocked site-wide” may instead describe the server’s 401 behavior, not robots.txt. | Medium | Split the claim into “robots policy” and “observed HTTP response.” Include the exact live robots text and a browser-UA versus generic-UA status comparison. |
| 10 | Yahoo’s fallback is not operationally specified. | The register names USA Today, AP, Reuters, AFP, and other syndication mirrors, but gives no tested mirror URLs, deduplication rule, or completeness estimate. [Yahoo robots.txt](https://news.yahoo.com/robots.txt) redirects to Yahoo’s robots file, which blocks many named AI agents with `Disallow: /` while leaving the generic group less restricted. | Medium | Add concrete mirror URLs and a collection rule. Label Yahoo as a syndication-discovery source, not an exhaustive outlet archive. |
